mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — captions across platforms and goals\n\nThese show the method in action: same brand, adapted properly per platform and goal — never\ncopy-pasted. Voice here is the blunt indie-founder voice from the voice-builder examples\n(short sentences, no exclamation marks, self-deprecating, lands on an imperative). Your output\nuses the actual brand's `voice.md`.\n\nAsset for examples 1–3: a screenshot of a feature being deleted from the product.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — Instagram · goal: saves · format: value/story\n\n> Deleted our most-requested feature today.\n>\n> Nobody used it. We just kept hearing about it in sales calls, so we built it. Six months,\n> gone in one commit.\n>\n> What we learned, so you don't repeat it:\n> – \"Requested\" isn't \"used.\" Watch behavior, not surveys.\n> – Build the boring thing people already do by hand.\n> – Shipping less is a feature.\n>\n> Save this before your next roadmap meeting.\n\n*First line wins the tap inside ~125 chars. Body pays off the hook. One CTA (save). On-voice:\nshort, no exclamation marks, dry. ~3 relevant hashtags would go at the end.*\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — LinkedIn · goal: comments · format: hot take\n\n> \"Requested\" is the most expensive word in product.\n>\n> We spent six months building a feature customers asked for in every sales call. We deleted it\n> today. Nobody used it.\n>\n> The ask was real. The need wasn't. People will tell you what sounds good and do something\n> else entirely — and if you build for the words instead of the behavior, you'll ship beautiful\n> things nobody touches.\n>\n> We're going to watch what people *do* now, not what they *say*.\n>\n> Curious if I'm wrong here — does anyone actually build straight from feature requests and have\n> it work?\n\n*Hook lands inside the ~140-char cutoff. Whitespace for dwell. Ends on a genuine question (the\ncomments goal). Link, if any, goes in comments.*\n\n---\n\n## Example 3 — X · goal: reach · format: contrarian (the post IS the caption)\n\n> \"Customers requested it\" is how you justify six months of building something nobody uses.\n>\n> Deleted ours today. Watching behavior from now on.\n\n*Two lines, the whole point up front. No hashtags. Punchy. Same idea, stripped to the platform.*\n\n---\n\n## Example 4 — same goal, two formats (Instagram · goal: comments)\n\nAsset: a photo of a messy desk.\n\n**Option A — question format**\n> 14 tabs open to avoid starting the one task that matters.\n>\n> Tell me I'm not the only one. What's your version of \"productive procrastination\"?\n\n**Option B — relatable/confession format**\n> My productivity system is opening more tabs until the real task feels less scary.\n>\n> It does not work. I do it every day anyway.\n\n*Recommend A if the goal is comments (it ends on an easy question); B if the goal is reach (more\nrelatable, more shareable). Both inside the cutoff, both on-voice. Showing two real alternatives\nbeats one take.*\n\n---\n\n## What every example shares\n\n- The **first line** works alone, inside the cutoff.\n- The body **pays off** the hook — no bait-and-switch.\n- **One** CTA, matched to the goal.\n- Mechanics (hashtags, links, length) fit the **platform**.\n- It **sounds like the brand** — fingerprint hit, \"never\" list respected.\n\nIf your draft misses any of these, fix the first line first, then the rest.\n"
